## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the fundamental understanding of combining data structures to achieve O(1) operations while maintaining an ordering constraint. It evaluates knowledge of hash map + linked list patterns and eviction logic commonly used in caching systems.

## Key Concepts
- HashMap for O(1) key-to-node lookups
- Doubly linked list for maintaining recency order
- Node manipulation for constant-time recency updates
- Edge case handling: missing keys, capacity limits, and duplicate operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class LRUCache {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.head = { prev: null, next: null };
    this.tail = { prev: null, next: null };
    this.head.next = this.tail;
    this.tail.prev = this.head;
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!this.cache.has(key)) return -1;
    const node = this.cache.get(key);
    this._moveToHead(node);
    return node.value;
  }

  put(key, value) {
    if (this.cache.has(key)) {
      const node = this.cache.get(key);
      node.value = value;
      this._moveToHead(node);
    } else {
      const newNode = { key, value, prev: null, next: null };
      this.cache.set(key, newNode);
      this._addToHead(newNode);
      if (this.cache.size > this.capacity) {
        this._removeTail();
      }
    }
  }

  _moveToHead(node) {
    this._removeNode(node);
    this._addToHead(node);
  }

  _addToHead(node) {
    node.prev = this.head;
    node.next = this.head.next;
    this.head.next.prev = node;
    this.head.next = node;
  }

  _removeNode(node) {
    node.prev.next = node.next;
    node.next.prev = node.prev;
  }

  _removeTail() {
    const node = this.tail.prev;
    this._removeNode(node);
    this.cache.delete(node.key);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a scalable data structure for tracking peak occupancy across a large domain with many overlapping intervals—a common problem in campaign management, resource allocation, and scheduling systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Implicit segment tree**: Enables efficient operations over a massive coordinate space (up to 2^31-1) without building a full tree
- **Range add, range max with argmax**: Combines lazy propagation for updates with maintaining both value and position of the maximum
- **Incremental updates**: Insert and erase operations dynamically modify the tree state
- **Handling duplicates**: Multiple intervals covering the same coordinate naturally accumulate via additive weights

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.max = 0;
    this.argMax = 0;
    this.lazy = 0;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}

function rangeAdd(node, L, R, ql, qr, delta) {
  if (qr < L || R < ql) return;
  if (ql <= L && R <= qr) {
    node.max += delta;
    node.lazy += delta;
    return;
  }
  push(node);
  const mid = (L + R) >>> 1;
  rangeAdd(node.left, L, mid, ql, qr, delta);
  rangeAdd(node.right, mid + 1, R, ql, qr, delta);
  pull(node);
}
```

